{
  "result": "At dawn, the city briefly forgets itself. Traffic lights blink over empty intersections, shopkeepers lift metal shutters, and pigeons claim the sidewalks as if they inherited them. In that quiet hour, ordinary places become slightly mysterious. A bakery smells like warmth escaping into the cold; a bus driver hums to the radio; someone, somewhere, waters a plant on a balcony no one else can see.\n\nI like this unclaimed portion of the day. It feels like a pause before the world remembers its appointments. During these minutes, attention becomes generous. You notice the cracked blue tile outside the pharmacy, the sparrow balancing on a wire, the old woman arranging oranges in a pyramid. None of these details demands anything, yet together they make a life.\n\nPerhaps this is why people keep journals, take photographs, or save ticket stubs. We are always trying to prove that our days contained more than errands. A small observation can rescue an afternoon from disappearing. Years later, memory rarely returns as a complete story. It arrives in fragments: cinnamon, rain against glass, a particular laugh. The fragment is enough. It opens a door, and behind it waits not the past exactly, but our former ability to inhabit it.\n\nTonight, when the city grows loud again, I will try to remember the morning without polishing it. Let its rough edges remain: the damp pavement, the impatient horn, the pigeon startled into flight. Meaning does not always need to be discovered. Sometimes it is simply what we noticed.",
